{
  "rationale": "The spec's throughput floor (1 MSPS) is trivially met by every family at these Fmax values (even iterative with N+3≈33 cycles clears it by a wide margin), so throughput is NOT the binding constraint. Accuracy is family-independent (identical bit-level datapath for the same W/N/guards/rounding), so the only real trade-off is area (luts_plus_ffs) vs accuracy_bits. That means the area-minimising family (iterative, one shared datapath) should dominate the front, and the useful exploration is over the accuracy boundary: W and N near the 2^-10 error limit, plus angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding which trade a little area for error headroom. Round 1 therefore spends most of the budget on iterative over a wide W/N window that brackets the feasible edge (W≈12-14, N≈11-14 expected), and gives smaller shares to unrolled_k and pipelined_m only to confirm they cannot undercut iterative on area and to catch any throughput-driven front segment. Ranges are deliberately wide in round 1 (W 10..18, N 8..20) so the NSGA-II can locate the feasible boundary; later rounds will narrow around whatever edge appears.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Smallest-area family: one shared micro-rotation datapath with barrel shifters, N+3 cycles/result. At ~100+ MHz and N<=20 this still gives >4 MSPS, so the 1 MSPS floor is never binding and area is free to be minimised. This is where the min-luts_plus_ffs selection rule will almost certainly land, and where the accuracy-vs-area front lives. Wide W/N window brackets the 2^-10 error edge; guards/rounding give the model room to trade a few LUTs for error headroom.",
