  "rationale": "Final round: constraints are comfortably satisfiable, so `infeasible` is ruled out — 280/340 evaluations are feasible, 0% violate the throughput_msps >= 1 constraint (best seen 171 MSPS), and the max_abs_err <= 0.000976562 constraint is met with large margin (best seen 5.51e-08 = 2^-24.11, and even the smallest-area front point at luts_plus_ffs=272 achieves 2^-10.78). The Pareto front is well populated (32 feasible designs, 252 unique) and spans both objectives broadly: luts_plus_ffs 272..687 against the HV reference 1500, and accuracy_bits 10.8..24.1 against the HV reference 10, with data_width 15..28 covering most of the registry's 8..28. Hypervolume is 1.494e+04 with only +1.7% gain this round, indicating the front has essentially converged. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is already served by the front's low-area corner: iterative [data_width=15 n_iter=15 angle_guard=4 frac_guard=1 rounding=trunc] at luts_plus_ffs=272, accuracy_bits=10.8, throughput 10.8 MSPS — meeting both constraints with margin. The unexplored `pipelined` family is a throughput-oriented variant (1 result/cycle) whose area would be far above the 272-point corner and is irrelevant to a min-area selection rule, so adding it cannot improve the selected design. Stopping now preserves the remaining 60 evaluations rather than spending them on a converged front.",
